@annevk 

> We discussed the relation of private fields and this proxy in https://github.com/tc39/ecma262/pull/688 and https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=1652868 and couldn't come up with a reasonable leak.

rewriting the OP example here:

```js
class Base {
  constructor(arg) {
    return arg;
  }
}

class ClassWithPrivateField extends Base {
  #field = 1;
  constructor(arg) {
    const {leak} = ClassWithPrivateField.prototype;
    super(arg).leak = leak.bind(this);
  }
  leak() {
    return this.#field;
  }
}

new ClassWithPrivateField(cross_origin_iframe.contentWindow);
cross_origin_iframe.contentWindow.leak();
// 1
```

does this address and raise concerns?

it's being discussed [in here](https://es.discourse.group/t/js-class-fields-potentially-harmful/1616) among other issues around fields.

@WebReflection My understanding is that the `HostEnsureCanAddPrivateElement` hook was added in response to an implementation bug because the seriousness of the bug & concern about similar implementation bugs in the future being possible was considered to outweigh the value of consistent behavior here — something like “let’s just remove the risk entirely because no one\* needs or is already using this capability”.

Private fields are a syntactic conceit that superficially reverses the map and key in a weakmap-like relation for ergonomic benefit. The “owner” of `#key` is the ES code whose PrivateEnvironment includes `#key` (any code evaluated in the class body’s scope). The `leak` function in the example could be calling `localWeakMap.get(this)` to realize the same relation & visibility. The risk being mitigated was that of hosts mis-implementing private field semantics when specific (very) exotic objects fraught with security-sensitive complexity were involved, not a risk stemming from private field semantics as they were actually specified.

@annevk (correct me if my assessment of the motivation for the change related to this issue is mistaken).

\* but me 🥲
